---
/**
 * Google Tag Manager — the id lives in `site.gtmId` and is GTM-5W69BTJQ, the
 * client's new container, issued 30 Sep 2026. It replaces GTM-5PRC4HBV, the
 * only tag the original site carried in its own markup (verified against the
 * 2026-09-18 archive capture: that id four times, and no `AW-`, `G-`, `UA-` or
 * UET id anywhere). One id, not five — the rest belongs inside the container.
 *
 * What the OLD container carried, for whoever builds out the new one. None of
 * it arrives with GTM-5W69BTJQ unless it is set up there:
 *
 *   Google Ads    AW-16819334998 — three conversion actions, two with Enhanced
 *                 Conversions reading the form fields, plus a remarketing tag
 *                 and a conversion linker (the piece that makes gclid
 *                 attribution survive)
 *   GA4           G-R27QW21PMT
 *   Microsoft Ads UET 187178776 — pageload plus custom events for form
 *                 submission and outbound clicks
 *   SearchKings   the agency's own template, account 535014 (the `SK` in the
 *                 original's comments)
 *
 * That is why Tag Assistant on the original lists three Google tags while the
 * page source contains one: it reports what loaded, not what was hard-coded.
 * Adding gtag directly alongside this would double-count every conversion.
 *
 * NOT COPIED, because it is not there: the specification's Section 4.2 says to
 * "retain ClickCease". There is no ClickCease script on any archived page and
 * no ClickCease tag in the container — there is nothing to retain. If the
 * client wants click-fraud filtering it is a new integration, not a migration.
 *
 * WHERE IT FIRES
 *
 * One guard, not two. An earlier version also required a build-time
 * `PUBLIC_ANALYTICS=1`, which `build:preview` did not set — so the staging
 * deploy shipped no tag at all and there was no way to confirm the port worked
 * short of going live. Two mechanisms for one decision is also two ways to ship
 * a silently untagged site, which is the bug this replaces.
 *
 * What remains is `site.analyticsHosts`: the container loads on the production
 * hostnames and on the staging hostname, and nowhere else. localhost and
 * *.workers.dev stay quiet, so a dev server cannot push anything into the
 * client's Google Ads account.
 *
 * Staging IS tagged, deliberately, and that has a cost worth knowing: its
 * pageviews land in the live GA4 property and its visitors enter the live
 * remarketing lists. Conversions do not follow from merely loading. Form
 * conversions fire on `lead_form_submission`, so testing the staging form
 * WILL register a conversion in the client's live Google Ads account. Don't,
 * unless that is the thing being tested.
 *
 * Phone conversions are a different mechanism (corrected in the SEO re-audit,
 * 7 Nov 2026; this comment used to say they fired on `click_to_call`). The
 * site does push `click_to_call` on every tel: tap (src/lib/phone-click.ts,
 * and LandingLayout.astro on the landing pages), but the live container, as
 * read on 7 Nov 2026, has no trigger on it: its only triggers are `gtm.init`,
 * `gtm.js` and `lead_form_submission`. Until the agency adds one, that event
 * records nothing. What records calls is the Google Ads "calls from website"
 * tag (`__awcc`, set up for "(416) 803-4880"): it swaps a Google forwarding
 * number into the page, and Ads counts calls made to that number. A tel: tap
 * made before the swap dials the real number and is never counted — which is
 * why paid visits load the container at once (WHEN IT LOADS, below). Staging
 * follows the same rule, so its /lp/ pages and any staging URL carrying a
 * click id load the container at once too; treat any number swapped in there
 * as live.
 *
 * `site_environment` is `production` on a PRODUCTION_HOSTS hostname and
 * `staging` on anything else the allowlist lets through. It used to test for
 * `10xconnections.com` in the hostname, which silently became `production`
 * the moment staging moved to stage.evergreencleaningservice.ca.
 *
 * It is pushed before `gtm.start` so it is readable by every
 * tag in the container. If the agency wants staging out of the data entirely,
 * that variable is the hook: a blocking trigger on
 * `site_environment equals staging` costs them one exception and needs no
 * change here.
 */
import { PRODUCTION_HOSTS, site } from '../data/site';
import { gtmLoaderScript } from '../lib/gtm-loader';

interface Props {
  /** Emits the `<noscript>` iframe half instead of the head loader. Goes
      first inside `<body>`, exactly where the original puts it. */
  noscript?: boolean;
}

const { noscript = false } = Astro.props;
const gtmId = site.gtmId;

/* Built as a string and written with `set:html` so what reaches the page is
   exactly these bytes. An earlier version wrapped an `is:inline` body in
   {`...`}; Astro emits inline script bodies as raw text, so the braces and
   backticks were published verbatim and the loader sat inside a template
   literal, on 76 pages, doing nothing. Anything that hides the emitted text
   behind a templating rule is a place that bug can come back. */
/* WHEN IT LOADS (SEO audit Phase 1, 6 Nov 2026; paid visits added in the
   SEO re-audit, 7 Nov 2026)

   The container is no longer fetched the moment the page starts. On the
   mobile homepage gtm.js (157 KB) and the gtag.js it pulls in (194 KB)
   blocked the main thread for about 1.3 s and competed with the hero image
   for bandwidth in the first second: Lighthouse mobile 51, TBT 1,316 ms. The
   site's own scripts are about 9 KB. Deferring the loader is the single
   largest performance change available without touching the container.

   On a paid visit it still is (see PAID VISITS, below). On every other
   visit the container now loads on whichever comes first:
     - the visitor's first pointerdown, keydown, touchstart or scroll, or any
       focus inside a form (someone about to fill the quote form);
     - a tap on any tel: link, which loads it immediately so the queued
       click_to_call push reaches the container. It does NOT save the call
       for Ads: the dialler opens on that same tap, before any number swap
       could happen (see PAID VISITS);
     - the window load event plus a short idle wait (requestIdleCallback with
       a 4 s ceiling, or a 4 s timer where it is not supported), so a visitor
       who reads without interacting is still measured.

   PAID VISITS (SEO re-audit, 7 Nov 2026). The deferral cost Google Ads its
   phone conversions. Calls are counted by the container's "calls from
   website" tag (`__awcc`), which swaps a Google forwarding number in for
   (416) 803-4880 only once gtm.js and gtag.js have loaded: 0.85-1.7 s into
   the visit in the Phase 4 lab runs, and later on a slow phone. A paid
   visitor who taps the header, hero or sticky-bar number before then dials
   the real number, and Ads never sees the call. So the container loads at
   parse time, exactly as the original snippet did, when the visit is paid:
     - the URL carries gclid, gbraid, wbraid or gad_source (an ad click);
     - the path starts with /lp/ (the landing pages exist for paid traffic);
     - a Google Ads click cookie is already set (`_gcl_aw`, `_gcl_gb` or
       `_gcl_ag`). The conversion linker writes these on the paid landing, so
       the second and third pages of the same paid visit, which have no click
       id in the URL, still get the swap before anyone can tap.
   Organic and direct visitors keep the deferral above, which is where the
   Lighthouse gain was measured; paid visitors pay the old cost so that
   their calls are counted.

   What is unchanged: `dataLayer` exists from the first byte, so every push
   the page makes before gtm.js arrives (site_environment, form events, the
   conversion event) is queued and processed when it does. The hostname guard
   and the staging/production variable are as before, and both run before
   either branch, so a paid-looking URL on localhost still loads nothing.

   The trade-off is a measurement one, agreed in the audit: page_view arrives
   a little later, and a visitor who leaves before the page has finished
   loading, without touching it, is not recorded. Watch Ads and GA4 for a
   week after this ships.

   WHAT IT ACTUALLY DOES, MEASURED (Phase 4, 6 Nov 2026; 25 Lighthouse runs,
   raw data summarised in evergreencleaningservice.ca-audit/PHASE-4-MONITORING.md).
   requestIdleCallback fires within milliseconds of `load` on an idle page,
   so the 4 s ceiling is never reached: gtm.js was requested 4-27 ms after the
   load event in every run (0.85-1.7 s into the visit on the lab profile),
   instead of at 0.49 s as before. That keeps the first second free for the
   hero image and fonts, which is where the lab gain came from (mobile home
   51 to 87, simulated LCP 6.8 s to 2.4 s). It does not remove the tags' own
   400-600 ms of main-thread work; that still happens, just after load. A
   longer fixed delay would remove more of it from the lab figure and lose
   more short visits from the analytics: a decision for whoever owns the Ads
   and GA4 numbers, not a code fix. Those figures hold for unpaid visits only:
   a lab run of an /lp/ page, or of any URL with a click id, now takes the
   paid branch and will look like the pre-Phase-1 numbers again. That is
   expected, not a regression.

   The loader text is built in src/lib/gtm-loader.ts (moved there in the SEO
   re-audit, 7 Nov 2026) so that tests/gtm-loader.test.ts can run the exact
   bytes this component writes in a DOM. */
const loader = gtmLoaderScript(gtmId, site.analyticsHosts, PRODUCTION_HOSTS);
---

{
  gtmId &&
    (noscript ? (
      <Fragment>
        {/* The original's own markup, minus SiteGround Speed Optimizer's
            lazy-load rewrite of the `src` — that is the host's artifact, not
            the tag. Unconditioned, as the original has it: without JavaScript
            there is no hostname check to run. */}
        <noscript
          set:html={`<iframe src="https://www.googletagmanager.com/ns.html?id=${gtmId}" height="0" width="0" style="display:none;visibility:hidden"></iframe>`}
        />
      </Fragment>
    ) : (
      <script is:inline set:html={loader} />
    ))
}
